 you  know back again hey he's not gonna play  and we got an even better chance the  fans didn't obviously understand the  magnitude of it but they quickly did  your thoughts with the fan reaction did  yeah I think you spot-on Dan I think  there were three chapters  what happened last night with the Durant  injury for starters you've got to go  back to even before the series began  we've been jokingly calling Durant John  wick on our show because he's just the  boogie man that's in the shadows and you  know if he shows up you're in big  trouble so there's been a lot of  trepidation  you know coursing through at the fan  base here knowing who he is and what he  represents it didn't help that he shows  up last night and hits two quick threes  and looks unbelievable in the first  quarter so there was a lot of angst  because of who Durant was and I think  when he originally got injured  the initial pop was people thought Serge  Ibaka got a steal now he didn't Durant  obviously blew out his Achilles or  whatever he did he just kind of let go  of the ball but you're right  I think it was human nature the first  three or four seconds like wow he's down  and that means the Raptors really have a  shot at winning this title that was the  first chapter the second chapter was the  ugliness where people I think understood  the extent of his injury by no means was  the 20,000 people I'm not sure what the  percentage of what of it was in terms of  the crowd but there it was loud enough  you could pick it up and they were  cheering the fact that this guy was  significantly injured  that was the second chapter the third  chapter though was Redemption I think  for Toronto and typically Canadian  because we had to apologize for our  initial reaction and people started  gentle his name and he got a huge  applause as he walked down the tunnel so  I don't think that third chapter has  been brought up enough I understand the  lure and the captivity that comes with  you know booing an injury and obviously  it leads to a lot of debate but there  was a real sense of you know sorrow for  Kevin Durant in the crowd and he did get  a pretty decent chant by the time you  left the building I mentioned this  yesterday in fact I think this was our  poll question for a little while  if I say to the you know the country of  Canada you can have a Raptors  championship or the Maple Leafs win  another Stanley Cup and I said I don't  think this is really a decision or a  choice like this is Canada winning a  championship you know people in Alberta  and Calgary couldn't care less about  Toronto winning a Stanley Cup but it  feels like  you know Canada would be able to embrace  a championship in a sport that they  haven't ever won a championship before  your thoughts on yeah absolutely there  are two quote unquote Canada's teams up  here  it's the Raptors and the Blue Jays  because they're on an island you know  they're the only Canadian teams in the  NBA and Major League Baseball we're in  the NHL you got seven Canadian teams and  the Maple Leafs are so hated by anyone  that isn't a vape relief and I would say  it's like the Yankees and the Cowboys  combined if somehow are you put together  the obnoxiousness of the Yankees fans  and cowboys fans they were dwarfed into  Maple Leaf fans without the success  although the Cowboys haven't had a lot  of success recently either so the rest  of the country it is so polarized do you  either love the Leafs or you vehemently  despise the leaves so I don't think  there's any question if you were if you  were casting votes throughout the  country  the Raptors winning a title would be a  landslide now in Toronto it'd be a very  different story you know obviously the  Raptors are big here but this is a Maple  Leaf town historically and I think it  still will be moving forward but from a  grassroots perspective I've been saying  recently I think if the Raptors can win  a title this will dwarf what Vince  Carter brought to this marke